{#
  Project facts as a label/value list — the "at a glance" column beside a case
  study's prose.

  Expects:
    items — a list of maps, each with a `label` and EITHER:
              value  — a finished string, rendered as-is; or
              values — a list of strings, rendered as individual items joined by
                       commas (the separator is CSS, see _meta.scss). Use this for
                       anything countable, so a long list can be capped: pass
                       `limit` alongside it and meta-more.js collapses the row to
                       that many with a "+N more" toggle.
            Rows with neither are dropped, so a caller can pass every row it knows
            about and let the ones without content fall away rather than testing
            each one first.

  EVERY item ships in the HTML, including the ones past `limit` — the collapse is
  done on mount by meta-more.js, never in CSS. So a crawler (and a visitor with JS
  off) gets the full list, exactly as the Read more control works.

  A <dl>, not a table or a stack of <p>s: these genuinely are term/description
  pairs, and the grouping <div> around each pair is valid inside a dl (it's what
  lets each row be styled as a unit).
#}
{# `?? null` on both, not a bare `i.value`: a row carries one key or the other, and
   under strict_variables reading the absent one is a runtime error, not an empty. #}
{% set items = (items ?? [])|filter(i =>
    (i.value ?? null) is not empty or (i.values ?? null) is not empty
) %}
{% if items %}
    <dl class="c-meta">
        {% for item in items %}
            <div class="c-meta__row">
                <dt class="c-meta__label">{{ item.label }}</dt>
                {% if (item.values ?? null) is not empty %}
                    {# The separator is REAL TEXT and it LEADS its item — ", Front-end",
                       not "Front-end, ". Two things fall out of that, and both are the
                       reason it isn't a CSS ::after:

                       No trailing comma, ever. Whichever item ends the visible run has
                       nothing after it. When the list is capped, meta-more.js adds its
                       own ", " before the "+N more" toggle — so the toggle reads as the
                       list's last item — and removes it again on reveal, so the open
                       list never ends on a dangling comma either. A separator baked in
                       here would need the last VISIBLE item found and stripped, which
                       moves as the row toggles.

                       And a crawler still reads the commas. It parses text nodes and
                       ignores generated content, so a ::after would hand it
                       "Front-endCraft CMSSupport" as one run-on token.

                       The spans are emitted tight against each other (`{%- -%}`): the
                       space after each comma lives INSIDE the span, which is also what
                       gives the line somewhere to wrap. #}
                    <dd class="c-meta__value"
                        {%- if item.limit ?? false %} data-meta-more="{{ item.limit }}"{% endif %}>
                        {%- for v in item.values -%}
                            <span class="c-meta__item">{{ loop.first ? '' : ', ' }}{{ v }}</span>
                        {%- endfor -%}
                    </dd>
                {% else %}
                    <dd class="c-meta__value">{{ item.value }}</dd>
                {% endif %}
            </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </dl>
{% endif %}
